{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1794443126,"nonce":"UZGXwjDByOYET/nPhfoA/yxC7/c=","message":"Visual harness, 2026-11-12 (1d43d299, local): crates/blinc_recorder/tests/animation_paths.rs.\n\nEach scene is replayed twice in a session of its own with a scripted timeline: once with a full paint every frame (the reference, right by construction) and once on the patched path a window takes (`snapshot_fast`: apply_binding_deltas, apply_css_deltas, blit). Frames are compared pixel for pixel and the test fails on any pixel off by more than two levels. `BLINC_ANIM_PATHS_OUT=\u003cdir\u003e` writes a contact sheet per scene (reference | painted | difference) at the first, last, worst and intermediate frames. The node table will be a third `Path`, held to the reference, not to the patched path.\n\nTwelve scenes: motion translate, scale, rotate and fade each alone, all four together, all four with no text, nested motion (an outer translate over an inner scale and turn), two overlapping children under one fading group, and CSS transitions and keyframe animations of transform and opacity, with and without text. 12 to 20 frames each.\n\nResult: the patched path matches the full paint exactly on all twelve (0 pixels differ over 2 levels, 0 channel difference, every frame). So the survey's worry about stale fields on the patched path (border width, shadow, clip) does not show on these scenes; the full paint draws them the same way. The scenes do move (I looked at the sheets): the cards translate, grow, turn and fade, and the overlapping group fades as one without the first child showing through the second.\n\nWhat the sheets showed instead is 40fc670: a rotate binding turns the box but not its label (except in the nested scene) and never its shadow, in the full paint and the patched path alike. The reference is therefore wrong for rotation of text and shadows, and the table cannot be held to it there. Decide what rotation does to glyphs and shadows (CSS: they turn) before the table goes live.\n\nNext: a Path for the table once the renderer has it, and scenes for what the table adds: text under an animated node (the fallback case that costs 48 ms on the desktop and kills the iPhone app, d20f077), sidebar and accordion opens, and a long list.","files":null}]}